                "entry_id": "31629911-4b05-384d-b7d5-4ce5374885c2",
                "word": "Cathay",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "263fd9ad-105e-3bd7-a736-a7686523d05b",
                        "entry_id": "31629911-4b05-384d-b7d5-4ce5374885c2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "China, specifically medieval northern China as reached by the overland Silk Road to Xi'an or Beijing, not known at the time to be related to southern China as reached by the maritime routes to Guangzhou."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "85e496f4-b09b-489b-894e-063db8bd1a14",
                                "sense_id": "263fd9ad-105e-3bd7-a736-a7686523d05b",
                                "text": "Toute la Chine eſt diviſée en ſeize Provinces qui valent mieux chacune que de grands Royaumes. Il y en a dix vers le Midi ; ſçavoir Yunnan, Quanſi, Canton, Fuquiem, Chequiam, NanKin, Kiamſi, Huqüam, Suſcüem & Quicheu. Les ſix vers le Septentrion ſont Xenſi, Scianſi, Honan, Xantung, PeKin & Leaotung, & c'eſt ce que pluſieurs appellent Cathai , au lieu qu’ils donnent le nom de Mangi aux Provinces Meridionales.",
                                "translation": "Le Monde, ou Géographie universelle, contenant la description et les cartes et les blasons des principaux pays du monde",
                                "english": "Le Monde, ou Géographie universelle, contenant la description et les cartes et les blasons des principaux pays du monde"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cfb23c64-82c9-4262-8281-b27bc0a1c3c8",
                                "sense_id": "263fd9ad-105e-3bd7-a736-a7686523d05b",
                                "text": "Columbus listened to many of these sea-stories, and heard many wonderful things about a very rich land away to the East that folks called Cathay.\nIf you look in your geographies you will not find any such place on the map as Cathay, but you will find China, and that was what men in the time of Columbus called Cathay. They told very big stories about this far-off Eastern land. They said its kings lived in golden houses, that they were covered with pearls and diamonds, and that everybody there was so rich that money was as plentiful as the stones in the street.\nThis, of course, made the sailors and storekeepers, who were part pirate, very anxious to go to Cathay and get some of the gold and jewels and spices and splendor for themselves. But Cathay was miles and miles away from Italy and Spain and France and England. It was away across the deserts and mountains and seas and rivers, and they had to give it up because they could not sail there."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3261bd57-b6d8-463b-973a-e342789c82ef",
                                "sense_id": "263fd9ad-105e-3bd7-a736-a7686523d05b",
                                "text": "For us that epic celebration somehow is confined to Thanksgiving Day when we celebrate the arrival of the Pilgrims who fled the tyranny of Charles I. But imagine, had the Genoese captain and his Spanish caravels not sailed eastward searching for Cipango and Cathay in 1492, then the Pilgrims might have been forced to turn to the known continents of Africa or Asia."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1986fa8f-fbb8-48c1-a659-61105ad7ddac",
                                "sense_id": "263fd9ad-105e-3bd7-a736-a7686523d05b",
                                "text": "For Marco Polo in the 13th century as for Ezra Pound in the 20th, China was \"Cathay,\" a land of mythic wonders. For the American-born director and writer Ping Chong, to visit China and make theater in the West meant exploring myth and history, joining Asian and Western traditions."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "491229f2-11b8-3191-b916-74b95e418ee1",
                        "entry_id": "31629911-4b05-384d-b7d5-4ce5374885c2",
